{
  "title": "Where are you?",
  "title_target": "Neredesin?",
  "goal": "Say where something is with the ending *-de*, and place things in relation to other things.",
  "bekal": [
    "choose between *-de*, *-da*, *-te* and *-ta* without hesitating",
    "name everyday places in a Turkish city",
    "use *yanında*, *karşısında*, *içinde* to describe where things are"
  ],
  "dialogs": [
    {
      "title": "Dialogue — Telefonda",
      "intro": "Budi calls Ayşe. He's standing somewhere in Kadıköy and has absolutely no idea where.",
      "lines": [
        {
          "speaker": "Ayşe:",
          "text": "Alo Budi, neredesin?",
          "id": "Hello, Budi, where are you?",
          "literal": "hello Budi, where-are-you?"
        },
        {
          "speaker": "Budi:",
          "text": "Bilmiyorum! Bir caddedeyim.",
          "id": "I don't know! I'm on a main road.",
          "literal": "not-knowing-I! a avenue-on-I-am."
        },
        {
          "speaker": "Ayşe:",
          "text": "Etrafında ne var? Bak biraz.",
          "id": "What's around you? Have a look.",
          "literal": "surroundings-your-in what there-is? look a-little."
        },
        {
          "speaker": "Budi:",
          "text": "Sağımda büyük bir cami var.",
          "id": "There's a big mosque on my right.",
          "literal": "right-my-on big a mosque there-is."
        },
        {
          "speaker": "Ayşe:",
          "text": "Caminin karşısında ne var?",
          "id": "What's across from the mosque?",
          "literal": "mosque-'s opposite-its-at what there-is?"
        },
        {
          "speaker": "Budi:",
          "text": "Bir banka ve bir eczane. Eczanenin yanında bir market.",
          "id": "A bank and a pharmacy. Next to the pharmacy there's a grocery store.",
          "literal": "a bank and a pharmacy. pharmacy-'s side-its-at a market."
        },
        {
          "speaker": "Ayşe:",
          "text": "Tamam, anladım. Moda'dasın.",
          "id": "OK, got it. You're in Moda.",
          "literal": "OK, understood-I. Moda-in-you-are."
        },
        {
          "speaker": "Budi:",
          "text": "Sen neredesin şimdi?",
          "id": "Where are you now?",
          "literal": "you where-are-you now?"
        },
        {
          "speaker": "Ayşe:",
          "text": "Okuldayım, sınıftayım. Ders var.",
          "id": "I'm at school, in the classroom. I have a class.",
          "literal": "school-at-I-am, classroom-in-I-am. lesson there-is."
        },
        {
          "speaker": "Budi:",
          "text": "Özür dilerim! Meşgul müsün?",
          "id": "Sorry! Are you busy?",
          "literal": "apology wish-I! busy question-you-are?"
        },
        {
          "speaker": "Ayşe:",
          "text": "Sorun değil. Deniz kenarında bir kafe var, orada buluşalım.",
          "id": "No problem. There's a café by the sea, let's meet there.",
          "literal": "problem not. sea edge-its-at a café there-is, there let-us-meet."
        },
        {
          "speaker": "Budi:",
          "text": "Harika. Ben kafedeyim o zaman.",
          "id": "Great. I'll be at the café, then.",
          "literal": "great. I café-at-I-am that time."
        }
      ],
      "notes": [
        "**Neredesin = nerede + sin.** The question word \"where\" already contains the ending *-de*: *ne-re-de*, literally \"at what place\". The person ending just attaches to it: *neredeyim*, *neredesin*, *nerede*, *neredeyiz*, *neredesiniz*.",
        "**Sorun değil, not sorun yok.** To brush off someone's apology, Turkish uses *sorun değil* (\"it's not a problem\") or *önemli değil* (\"it's not important\"). Both are equally polite and equally common.",
        "**Place words take the possessive first, then -de.** *Karşı* means \"opposite\", but \"across from the mosque\" becomes *caminin karşısında*: mosque-'s + opposite-its + at. Three endings stacked, always in that order.",
        "**Bak means \"look\" as a command.** It's also used like \"hey, listen\" to get attention: *Bak, sana bir şey söyleyeceğim* — \"Listen, I'm going to tell you something.\""
      ]
    }
  ],
  "sections": [
    {
      "title": "The ending -de: in, at, on",
      "blocks": [
        {
          "type": "p",
          "text": "The location ending has **four forms**: two from vowel harmony and two more from consonant harmony. That second part is new — and it applies to many more endings later on."
        },
        {
          "type": "tabel",
          "head": [
            "Word ends in",
            "Last vowel a, ı, o, u",
            "Last vowel e, i, ö, ü"
          ],
          "rows": [
            [
              "a soft letter (everything else)",
              "**-da** — okulda, Ankara'da",
              "**-de** — evde, İzmir'de"
            ],
            [
              "a hard letter f s t k ç ş h p",
              "**-ta** — kitapta, uçakta",
              "**-te** — işte, derste"
            ]
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "kotak",
          "title": "FiSTiKÇi ŞaHaP",
          "items": [
            "Turkish's eight hard consonants — **f, s, t, k, ç, ş, h, p** — are memorized by Turkish schoolchildren as one made-up name: *Fıstıkçı Şahap*, \"Şahap the pistachio seller\".",
            "If a word ends in one of those eight letters, an ending starting with *d* changes to *t*: *kitap-ta*, *iş-te*, *uçak-ta*, *genç-ten*.",
            "Learn the made-up name now. It'll come in handy again in Unit 8 (*-den*) and Unit 11 (*-di*)."
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "sub",
          "text": "What it's used for"
        },
        {
          "type": "tabel",
          "head": [
            "Example",
            "English",
            "Note"
          ],
          "rows": [
            [
              "Evdeyim.",
              "I'm at home.",
              "place"
            ],
            [
              "Kitap masada.",
              "The book is on the table.",
              "place"
            ],
            [
              "Saat üçte.",
              "At three o'clock.",
              "time"
            ],
            [
              "Bende para yok.",
              "I don't have any money on me.",
              "\"on me\""
            ],
            [
              "Türkiye'de yağmur var.",
              "It's raining in Turkey.",
              "country"
            ],
            [
              "Ne iş yapıyorsun, nerede?",
              "What do you do, and where?",
              "question"
            ]
          ]
        }
      ]
    },
